<% @page_title = "Using with AI" %>
<% client, clients = term(:client).downcase, term(:client, count: 2).downcase %>
<% engagement, engagements = term(:engagement).downcase, term(:engagement, count: 2).downcase %>
<% work = term(:work).downcase %>
<% agent_name = Current.user.agent&.display_name || "your agent" %>

<%= render "settings/header", current: :help_ai %>

<div class="panel panel--wide shadow center txt-align-start flex flex-column gap">
  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">Talk to it like a colleague</h2>
    </header>
    <p>
      Once your assistant is connected (<%= link_to "Connecting an MCP", settings_help_mcp_path, class: "txt-link" %>), you don’t need to know Runwell’s screens or ids.
      Say what you want, in your words, and it finds the <%= clients %>, <%= engagements %> and people by name. When a name could mean two things, it asks.
    </p>
    <p class="txt-small txt-subtle margin-none">
      It works as <strong><%= agent_name %></strong> with your role, and shows you anything that would reach a <%= client %> before doing it. It finishes <%= work %> into <em>In review</em>; a person marks it done.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">Ready-made starts</h2>
    </header>
    <p class="txt-small">Most assistants list these as prompts or shortcuts once Runwell is connected:</p>
    <ul class="help-tree txt-small">
      <% Agent::Prompts::STAFF.each do |prompt| %>
        <%# herb:key prompt.name %>
        <li><strong><%= prompt.title %></strong> <span class="txt-subtle"><%= prompt.description %></span></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">If you run projects</h2>
    </header>
    <ul class="help-tree txt-small">
      <li>“What needs me today? Most urgent first.”</li>
      <li>“Catch me up on the <em>Spanish pages</em> <%= engagement %>: what’s agreed, done, in progress, and waiting on the <%= client %>.”</li>
      <li>“Give the <em>holiday hours</em> <%= work %> to Priya, due Friday.”</li>
      <li>“Triage the open requests: for each, suggest what it should become, and ask me before promoting any.”</li>
      <li>“Add <em>Spanish pages</em> as a change order on <em>WO-12</em> and send it to <em>Mia</em>.” <span class="txt-subtle">It shows you the change order first.</span></li>
      <li>“Draft this week’s update for <em><%= client %></em> from what happened. Don’t send it.”</li>
      <li>“Before my call with <em><%= client %></em>, summarise everything on their account since last week.”</li>
      <li>“Find overdue <%= work %> and commitments, and draft a note to each owner.”</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">If you do the work</h2>
    </header>
    <ul class="help-tree txt-small">
      <li>“What am I on today?”</li>
      <li>“The hero images are done: put them in review and note what changed.”</li>
      <li>“Start on my next <%= work %>.” <span class="txt-subtle">On any piece of <%= work %>, <kbd>c</kbd> or the ✦ button copies a brief your assistant can work from: the agreed scope, notes, and how to report back.</span></li>
      <li>“Ask Sarah whether Sunday hours are in scope.” <span class="txt-subtle">It leaves a note that mentions her.</span></li>
      <li>“Check my in-progress <%= work %> against the code and move what’s actually finished to In review, with a note saying how you know.”</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">On a schedule</h2>
    </header>
    <p class="txt-small">Assistants that run tasks for you (a morning summary, a Friday check) can ask Runwell only what’s changed since they last looked, so they never repeat themselves.</p>
    <ul class="help-tree txt-small">
      <li>“Every weekday at 8, message me what changed in Runwell overnight and what needs me today.”</li>
      <li>“Every Friday afternoon, list <%= engagements %> with nothing done this week, and anything overdue.”</li>
      <li>“When a new request comes in from <em><%= client %></em>, tell me, with your suggestion for what it should become.”</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">Your <%= clients %>’ assistants</h2>
    </header>
    <p class="txt-small">
      <%= clients.upcase_first %> who connect their own assistant to their portal can ask it “Where are things at?”, “What’s waiting on me?” or “Ask them for a Spanish menu by Friday”, and it comes to you as a request, marked as sent through their assistant.
      Requests also arrive by email when the requests address is set in <%= link_to "Settings → Email", settings_email_path, class: "txt-link" %>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="settings__section">
    <header>
      <h2 class="divider">Staying in control</h2>
    </header>
    <ul class="help-tree txt-small">
      <li><strong>See what it did</strong>: each connection in <%= link_to "Connected apps", settings_connected_apps_path, class: "txt-link" %> lists its recent calls, and the history on every record says what came through an assistant.</li>
      <li><strong>Pause or disconnect</strong> any connection there, at once. An owner can for anyone’s, <%= clients %>’ included.</li>
      <li><strong>Read only</strong>: connect with read-only access when it should look and not change anything.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing reaches a <%= client %> unasked</strong>: sending, emailing and sharing always wait for your yes.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
